OK.  It seems clear to me that you don't actually understand how the
whole sacrificing thing works in the first place. I am NOT going to provide 
full numeric spoilers; that can be done by someone who chooses to dive into 
the code.  But I can say a few things, and perhaps you'll understand why
I'm going to wait for someone to _SHOW ME_ that there's a problem,
rather than speculating endlessly about hypothetical full suits of +5
armor on DL1.

When you sacrifice, a number of criteria are checked for your character.
Eventually, you reach the point where you aren't negative luck, god
isn't mad, prayer timeout doesn't need fixing, etc. etc., and the game 
decides to see whether god wants to give you something.

Now, god can give you a "gift" or an "artifact".  When you start out,
the scale is slanted entirely towards "gift".  As you increase in XL,
it starts to tip more and more towards the "artifact" side, until
eventually there is no chance that god will even consider a "gift"
anymore.

Once god has selected whether you're on the "gift" or "artifact" track,
the game then makes some computations as to whether you'll get anything
_AT ALL_.  This is separate from determining whether it's a gift or an
artifact in the first place; if the original computation puts you on the
"artifact" track, you CANNOT receive a gift at this point in the code!

What this means practically is that as you increase in XL, your chances
of gifts start to decrease in a manner unrelated to how many gifts
you've already received.  When you're sacrificing, you're killing things
to make corpses, which tends to increase your XL, which means that even
getting nothing at all is (slowly) reducing your chances of getting a gift.

If you want to avoid increasing in XL...

>> You can rely on your pet to make corpses, of course, but unless you've
>> got a pony, your pet also tends to EAT those corpses -- so now we've
>> added one more prerequisite onto the list, reading "Start as a Knight".

... Oh, wait, I already typed that, I see.  I guess it WASN'T a
strawman, was it, and was instead a pre-emptive answer to "what if I
avoid increasing my XL?"

Now, back to the main line.  Part of the second computation for whether
you get anything at all at this point is a check on how many gifts or
artifacts you've already received; the more you've gotten, the less likely 
you are to get another one at this point, and this sacrifice will just
end up being a luck-increaser.  Once god has decided whether you'll get
something in the first place, THEN it goes off and picks an appropriate
thing at random (according to the generation probabilities for those
items).

Do you understand now why getting a javelin and orcish cloak can start
to mess up your chances of getting something good?  Not only does it
affect you directly (more gifts == less chance that any given sacrifice
will be a gift), it affects you indirectly (more time spent sacrificing
== higher XL due to killing == less chance that you'll even be on the
gift track anymore).

We haven't even started to discuss the logistics of altar camping, none
of which are particularly trivial in the early game (food is a biggie;
corpses that you're saccing aren't corpses you're eating) and could lead
to your not having time to sit and wait for long enough to get more than
a couple gifts.

So yes, the fact that you could get cruft _does_ matter, because this is
not Vacuum Nethack.  There are a lot of other things going on besides
sitting there banging on your altar to see what you get.

